     <h3>Backups and restored data</h3>
     <p>Include backup copies in the retention plan. Where the agreed policy allows them to await expiry, define the restrictions while they remain, their expiry and the evidence that expiry occurred. Deleting live rows does not remove an older backup’s contents. PostgreSQL’s ordinary deletion also need not immediately remove an old physical row version; logical absence and removal from every stored copy are different milestones.</p>
-    <p>A restore can bring deleted information back. Recovery must carry forward the still-applicable deletion decisions and apply them before the restored data returns to ordinary use. Preserve enough controlled information to do that without retaining the whole removed payload. The same check belongs before rebuilding search indexes or documents from an old source. Backup selection, restore procedures and reconciliation have their own scope in the planned <em>Backup, restore and reconciliation</em> article.</p>
